Create kernel/compatibility_layer.py:

"""OS Compatibility Layer — Formal verification of OS invariants.

Kingdom OS is a specification, not a replacement. This module defines
what it means for an existing OS (Linux, macOS, Windows, BSD, Redox,
seL4, Fuchsia) to satisfy Kingdom OS invariants.

The compatibility layer checks:
1. POSIX compliance (file operations, process management)
2. Capability security (can the OS enforce POLA?)
3. Determinism (given same inputs, same outputs?)
4. Glass-box (are all state transitions inspectable?)
5. Consent (is all authority delegated, never assumed?)

A system scores on each axis. Full compliance = Kingdom OS aligned.
Partial compliance = specific gaps identified with remediation paths.

Biblical: Romans 13:1 — "Let everyone be subject to the governing
authorities, for there is no authority except that which God has
established."
"""

Implement:

1. OSProfile:
   @dataclass
   name: str  # "linux", "macos", "windows", "bsd", "redox", "sel4", "fuchsia"
   version: str
   posix_compliant: bool
   has_capability_model: bool
   has_mandatory_access_control: bool
   has_audit_trail: bool
